        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Yayoi culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Japanese archipelago, beginning in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and spreading to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>; Yayoi traits are strongest in Kansai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. Key sites: Itazuke and Nabata (northern Kyushu), Yoshinogari (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saga-prefecture">Saga Prefecture</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makimuku">Makimuku</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> Prefecture) and the Yayoi district of Tokyo.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Conventionally c. 300 BCE – 300 CE, ending in the mid-3rd century CE with the first keyhole-shaped burial mounds; radiocarbon dates, still highly controversial, may place its start as early as c. 1000–800 BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming in paddy fields; plain, practical pottery; bronze ceremonial bells (dōtaku), mirrors and swords; iron tools and weapons; raised-floor dwellings; moated and hilltop settlements; Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himiko">Himiko</a> and Yamataikoku</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated settlements, paddy fields and burials; radiocarbon dating of residue on pottery; comparisons of skeletons; Chinese histories (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Book of the Later Han</a> and the Wei zhi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a>); the study of the Japonic languages.</span></div>
        </div>
