    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Yayoi period brought wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming to the Japanese archipelago, and with it permanent villages, stored grain, landholding and a more stratified society.</li><li>Farming migrants from the Korean Peninsula and other parts of the continent mixed with the Jōmon population, laying the foundations of the modern Japanese people and, most linguists think, spreading the Japonic languages.</li><li>Chinese histories describe the land of Wa as many scattered communities, among them the Na state, which received a golden seal in 57 CE, and Yamataikoku under Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himiko">Himiko</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The golden seal that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu</a> of the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> gave to the Na state of Wo in 57 CE, recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Book of the Later Han</a>, was found in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> in the 18th century.</p></aside>
    </section>

